    (property "Citation" "[43]" (id 5) (at 0 0 0)
      (effects (font (size 1.27 1.27)) hide)
    )
    (property "Verification" "VERIFIED (pin numbers) -- every physical pin number below is read directly from the local copy of [43] (TI MSPM0G3507-Q1/G3506-Q1/G3505-Q1 datasheet, SLASF88C, October 2023, revised September 2025, 126 pp.), '28 DGS28' column of Table 6-3 'Signal Descriptions' (pp. 17-25), cross-checked against the '28 VSSOP' column of Table 6-2 'Pin Attributes' (pp. 13-15) and against the package pin diagram, Figure 6-7 '28-Pin DGS28 (VSSOP) (Top View)' (p. 12). All three agree; the symbol carries all 28 physical pins, so it is a complete package-exact part, not a subset like the superseded 64-pin symbols/specs/MSPM0G3507.json ([1]) was. PACKAGE SELECTION RATIONALE (AGENTS.md SS4 judgment call, not a datasheet requirement): DGS28 is the smallest of the six packages this family ships in ([43] p.1 'Package options'; p.2-3 package-size table gives 28 VSSOP = 7.1 x 3 mm, versus 32 VSSOP 8.1 x 4.9 mm, 32 VQFN 5 x 5 mm, 48 VQFN 7 x 7 mm, 48 LQFP 9 x 9 mm, 64 LQFP 12 x 12 mm). It carries 24 GPIO ([43] Table 5-1, p.7) against the 21 signals this build's MCU role requires (see symbols/specs/S32K144.json for the same 21-signal role list: 2 CAN, 3 RS-485, 4 gate-driver SPI, 4 ADC, 6 PWM, 2 SWD), so DGS28 meets the requirement with 3 GPIO to spare and no larger package is needed. SIGNAL-TO-PIN ASSIGNMENT is likewise this build's own design decision among the IOMUX alternatives Table 6-2 lists for each pin (AGENTS.md SS4), NOT a datasheet-mandated mapping; each pin's 'mux' field below records the exact datasheet function name and PINCM function index [n] it relies on, so any reassignment can be re-checked against Table 6-2/6-3 directly. Three assignments are forced by the package, not chosen: CAN_TX and CAN_RX have exactly one DGS28 pin each (pins 1 and 2 -- PA12/PA13, which carry CAN on the larger packages, are not bonded out on DGS28), and SWDIO/SWCLK have exactly one pin each (22/23). CONSTRAINTS THIS ASSIGNMENT DELIBERATELY HONORS, each a judgment call: (a) all six PWM outputs come from advanced-control timer TIMA0 as three complementary pairs (C0/C0N, C1/C1N, C2/C2N), which is the only way to get the hardware dead-band insertion a 3-phase inverter half-bridge needs -- [43] SS8.22, p.63 area, lists 'Complementary output PWM' and 'Asymmetric PWM with programmable dead band insertion' for TIMA0/TIMA1; (b) the three phase-current inputs are split across both ADC instances (ADC_IU=A0_7 and ADC_IV=A0_3 on ADC0, ADC_IW=A1_0 on ADC1) so two conversions can run concurrently on the two 12-bit ADCs rather than serially on one -- whether that split is sufficient for this build's chosen control-loop topology is an open question, since the control axis is still unspecified (builds/6s/50A/CAN_485_faraday/README.md 'Open items'); (c) pin 13 (PA6) is left unassigned specifically because it is one of only two DGS28 pins that can carry TIMA_FAL0, the advanced timer's hardware fault-shutdown input (Table 6-3, p.24: TIMA_FAL0 DGS28 = pins 1, 13 -- and pin 1 is forced to CAN_TX), so wiring DRV8353S nFAULT to a hardware PWM-shutdown path remains physically possible on this package; that wiring is NOT decided here, because builds/6s/50A/CAN_485_faraday/kicad/README.md explicitly holds nFAULT open as an unresolved design question and assigning it in this symbol would resolve it by fiat; (d) pin 21 (PA18) is left unassigned because it is the ROM bootloader's hardware invoke pin (Table 6-3, p.17: BSL_invoke DGS28 = pin 21, sole location, not relocatable), so the BSL field-update entry path stays available. TRADE-OFF ACCEPTED, flagged rather than hidden: ADC_VBUS is assigned to pin 24, which is also VREF- (Table 6-3, p.25). Consuming VREF- rules out an external ADC voltage reference and commits the design to the internal shared reference ([43] p.1 'Features': 'Configurable 1.4V or 2.5V internal shared voltage reference (VREF)'; SS7.15/SS8.14). The alternative -- ADC_VBUS on pin 21 (A1_3), keeping VREF+/VREF- both free -- was rejected because it consumes the non-relocatable BSL_invoke pin, and because the INA240 current-sense outputs this ADC reads are ratiometric to their own supply and the VREF_MID bias rail, which favors a supply-referenced conversion over an absolute external reference. This is a one-line reversal in this file if the repo owner prefers the other side of the trade. NOT VERIFIED / NOT CLAIMED: the GPIO (plain digital I/O) PINCM function index is not enumerated in [43] -- Table 6-2 lists each pin's alternate functions starting at index [2] and footnote (1) states only that PF and PC are set to 0 for analog functions -- so RS485_DE_RE's 'mux' field below names the function without asserting an index; see [39] (SLAU846E) IOMUX chapter to pin that down. This datasheet also documents no hardware RS-485 driver-enable mode for any UART ([43] SS8.23 Table 8-15 'UART Features', p.69, lists LIN/DALI/IrDA/ISO7816/Manchester for UART0 only and no RS-485 entry for any instance), which is why RS485_DE_RE is a plain GPIO here rather than a hardware-driven DE -- the same treatment symbols/specs/S32K144.json gives it. Adopting this part into a build additionally requires a MSPM0-specific power/analog review that this symbol does not perform (VCORE decoupling, VREF decoupling per Table 6-3 footnote (3), and the unused-pin terminations in Table 6-4, p.25)." (id 6) (at 0 0 0)
      (effects (font (size 1.27 1.27)) hide)
    )
